her validates their clinical utility and safety. The presence of both acute and preventive indications underscores the versatility and importance of this drug class within the community [Data: Entities (138, 145, 146, 149, 150, 151, 152, 154, 156, 509, 510, 511, 512, 513, 514, 515, 516, 517, 518, 519, 520, 521, 522, 523, 524, 525, 526, 527, 528, 529); Relationships (173, 186, 187, 189, 190, 191, 192, 195, 209, 691, 694, 698, 705, 707, 708, 709, 712, 713, 717, 720, 721, 722, 724, 725, 726, 729, 732, 736, 737, +more)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Gepants are a novel, targeted class of drugs for acute and preventive migraine treatment\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"NSAIDs and acetaminophen form the foundation of first-line treatment for mild to moderate acute migraine attacks. These non-specific analgesics are widely used due to their accessibility, safety profile, and effectiveness in reducing pain and associated symptoms. NSAIDs such as ibuprofen, naproxen, diclofenac, and aspirin are recommended with specific dosing guidelines to prevent medication overuse headache (MOH). For example, ibuprofen is dosed at 200–400mg, naproxen at 500mg, and diclofenac at 50–100mg, with maximum daily limits to ensure safety. Acetaminophen (paracetamol) is also a first-line option, with a typical effective dose of 1000mg and a maximum daily dose of 4000mg. These medications are integral to the Stepped Treatment approach, where they are used as initial interventions before progressing to more targeted therapies. Their inclusion in the Non-Specific Treatment category reflects their broad mechanism of action, which does not target migraine-specific pathways. Despite their widespread use, they are not without risks, particularly when overused, which can lead to MOH. The strong relationship between NSAIDs and acetaminophen with the Stepped Treatment framework underscores their role as initial therapeutic steps. The FDA’s recognition of these drugs as safe and eff